    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's a good start. i want to get even more granular for them. once that switch is flipped, how does it actually translate to the physical protein structures being built? specifically, how does the mTORC1 activation lead to the phosphorylation of those downstream targets like S6K1 or 4E-BP1? i'm trying to show the actual pathway from the leucine signal to the ribosome.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The activation of mTORC1 by leucine is a cascade of phosphorylation events that directly impacts the translational machinery. When mTORC1 is active, it acts like a foreman, aggressively phosphorylating its downstream targets. It phosphorylates S6 Kinase 1 ($\\text{S6K1}$), which in turn promotes the translation of specific ribosomal proteins, essentially speeding up the assembly line. Simultaneously, mTORC1 phosphorylates Eukaryotic Initiation Factor $4\\text{E-Binding Protein }1$ ($\\text{4E-BP1}$), causing it to release the $\\text{eIF4E}$ factor, which is essential for cap-dependent mRNA translation at the ribosome.\n\nThis dual action—activating $\\text{S6K1}$ and freeing $\\text{eIF4E}$—is what drives the ribosomes to rapidly synthesize new muscle proteins. It's a finely tuned, enzymatic process that translates a simple amino acid signal into tangible, physical growth. This whole pathway is a beautiful example of cellular regulation.",
      "source": "target",
